course: dictionary
lesson: 8006
title: Redundancy: three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Redundancy is a word with three meanings, and the context tells you which one you need. At work, redundancy means losing your job because the employer no longer needs that job, such as when a factory closes. It is not the worker's fault.
explain: «Redundancy means the job itself is being got rid of, not that the person did anything wrong.» The sentence tells you the reason, so you know it is not a punishment. Check by swapping in your meaning: "the job is no longer needed" fits the sentence.
explain: Money can also be called redundancy. Redundancy pay is given to a worker who is made redundant. Phrases like «voluntary redundancy» and «face redundancy» show that the word is about jobs and money.
explain: The third meaning is having more of something than is needed, like a spare part kept just in case. Engineers see this as a good thing, because a spare engine or backup computer keeps things safe. In writing, saying the same thing twice is also called redundancy.
fact: Redundancy means losing your job because the employer no longer needs that job.
fact: Redundancy is not the same as being sacked for doing something wrong.
fact: Redundancy pay is money given to a worker who is made redundant.
fact: Redundancy can also mean having more of something than is needed, like a spare backup computer.
fact: Useful phrases: be made redundant, face redundancy, voluntary redundancy.
exercise: «Redundancy means the job itself is being got rid of, not that the person did anything wrong.» What does this tell you? | *The job is no longer needed, so the worker is not to blame | It is a punishment for bad work | The worker wants to leave | The worker is too lazy to keep it
exercise: Mia reads: «The factory closed, so many workers were made redundant.» What is happening to them? | They are going on a holiday | *They are losing their jobs because the jobs are no longer needed | They are moving to a new town for fun | They are being promoted
exercise: A writer wrote «a free gift» and then «a gift that costs nothing» in the same line. What is the problem? | *It is redundancy, because it says the same thing twice | It is redundancy pay | It is a job loss | It is a spare engine
test: What is redundancy pay? | Pay for doing extra hours at work | Money given to people on holiday | *Money given to a worker who is made redundant | A spare engine kept just in case
test: Which is an example of having more than is needed? | A factory closing and workers losing their jobs | A job that is never boring | *A backup computer kept just in case | Money given to a worker who leaves
exam: Jayden's dad says, «I was made redundant, but it was not my fault.» What does made redundant mean here? | *Lost a job because the employer no longer needed it | Got a big promotion at work | Won a prize for good work | Did something wrong at work
